# Design: Bounded reads for ACL-filtered list queries (Workstream 3)

## Problem
The dashboard list queries used unbounded .collect(): videos.list
read every video in a project plus every comment per video,
projects.list and teams.listWithProjects read every project and
every video per project just to compute counts. Upstream lawn fixed
this in 62c0463 (.take(101) count caps) and aaf96d8 (capped-count
flags), but upstream has no per-video ACL. The fork filters every
video through the restricted-access grant check first, so a naive
.take() before filtering would let inaccessible videos consume the
cap.

## Approach
One shared cap (LIST_ITEM_CAP = 100, take 101) in convex/listCaps.ts.
- ACL-filtered reads (videos.list, listPlaybackTargetsForProject
  without videoIds, per-project accessible counts) page the index
  with .paginate() and collect until 101 accepted items prove
  truncation or the index is exhausted: cap AFTER filtering.
- listPlaybackTargetsForProject with explicit videoIds does one
  ctx.db.get per unique requested id. The request bounds the read,
  so no cap applies; project scoping and the ACL are checked per id.
- Reads with no per-item ACL (projects by team, memberships by user)
  use the plain .take(cap + 1).
- The grant set makes each ACL check a pure set lookup
  (canTeamMemberAccessVideoWithGrantSet), so filtering adds no
  per-video queries inside the paged loop.

## Key Trade-offs
- Worst case (every video restricted) still scans the whole project
  range, same as the old .collect(); it is never worse, and usually
  stops after one page. maximumRowsRead was considered and rejected:
  it would silently truncate the accessible set.
- Return shapes stay arrays (clients unchanged); truncation is
  reported via console.warn at each site and additive
  commentCountIsCapped / videoCountIsCapped item flags instead of a
  hasMore envelope, which would have required client edits outside
  this workstream's file ownership.
- Comment counts cap at 100 (upstream used 200) for one consistent
  cap across every site.
- Delete cascades (videos.remove, projects.remove, teams.deleteTeam)
  stay unbounded on purpose: capping them would leak orphan rows.

